WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.670
Speaker 1: We're continuing our interviews
with all sorts of different 2022 candidates.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:09.670
And today I'm going to be speaking with Adam
Stein, who's a self-described America first

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:15.350
candidate, endorsed by the former president,
Donald Trump, currently running a write in

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:20.840
campaign in the 63rd District for the Wisconsin
State Assembly.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.060
Adam, I really appreciate your time today.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.980
Speaker 3: Thanks for having me, David.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:25.980
I appreciate it.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.189
Speaker 1: So indulge me.

00:00:27.189 --> 00:00:28.189
I know this.

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:31.760
We may get through this first question in
one word and then we can move on.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.330
But it's just a good gut check.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.680
Who is the current president of the United
States?

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.600
Speaker 3: Donald Trump.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.480
Speaker 1: Oh, the current president is Trump.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:41.950
Speaker 3: No.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:42.950
Right.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:43.950
Right now it's Joe Biden, sir.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:44.950
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:49.110
Did he legitimately when do you think does
his presidency reflect the will of the voters?

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:54.490
Speaker 3: I believe, especially here in the
state of Wisconsin, we have some major questions

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:58.600
that we'd like to ask, and we have not been
able to bring those before a judge here in

00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.600
the state of Wisconsin.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.460
Speaker 1: Who is preventing you from doing
that?

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:08.850
Speaker 3: Right now, the only person that
we can find is my opponent, Robin Barnes.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:13.729
He has said multiple times that he is going
to do a full forensic cyber audit when he

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:18.120
got off the plane with the president back
August of last year.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:23.810
And he has done absolutely nothing of the
sort in order to find out really what people

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:24.810
want to know, David.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.049
They want to know that their ballot counts,
their vote is actually counted.

00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:29.049
That's all they want to know.

00:01:29.049 --> 00:01:34.000
Speaker 1: And what reason do we have right
now to question the results in which Joe Biden

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.649
won that state by 20,000 votes?

00:01:36.649 --> 00:01:38.299
What specifically do we have?

00:01:38.299 --> 00:01:40.020
Speaker 3: In Wisconsin.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.020
Yes.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.020
Let's go.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:48.270
JOHNSON So we have the Supreme Court announcing
their ruling back about a month and a half

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:54.329
ago that the ballot drop boxes are and were
illegal.

00:01:54.329 --> 00:01:58.430
There's two methods of delivering your ballot
in the state of Wisconsin Constitution.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:04.469
By statute, you can deliver it in the mail
or you can deliver it in person to the clerk.

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:09.369
The Wisconsin Election Commission decided
to add a third option, which is a ballot drop

00:02:09.369 --> 00:02:12.730
box, which is not clearly defined in the statute.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:18.930
So when the Supreme Court ruled, they said
those drop boxes are and were illegal.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.870
So there were about 250,000 votes cast.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:22.560
Speaker 1: But Adam, hold on a second.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.560
Hold on.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:24.560
I know.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:25.560
I know.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:26.640
You're two too good to do this to us.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:33.680
You know that if you read that decision, what
they found was that there were some locations

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:39.050
that were not accessible to individuals with
mobility issues.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:45.470
And they did they said nothing substantive
about fraud related to the final count or

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:46.470
anything of the sort.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:47.590
Speaker 3: No, no, no, no.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:49.239
I was simply giving you the reasoning.

00:02:49.239 --> 00:02:50.239
You're exactly right.

00:02:50.239 --> 00:02:52.110
I would not mean to admit that.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.150
Speaker 2: Okay.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.150
Fair.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:55.150
Fair.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:56.150
Speaker 3: Very correct, David.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.150
Very, very correct.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.010
The amount of ballots that were cast by that
method, in lieu of the fact that it doesn't

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:04.480
follow the method to deliver the ballot by
the Constitution.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:05.719
Yeah.

00:03:05.719 --> 00:03:09.860
Would in my case bring to question the fact
that those ballots, whether legal or not,

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.769
were cast in an unlawful manner.

00:03:11.769 --> 00:03:12.769
You're exactly right.

00:03:12.769 --> 00:03:13.769
Okay.

00:03:13.769 --> 00:03:18.090
Speaker 1: And do you also admit that while
it is totally within your right to extrapolate

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:23.910
whatever you want based on that decision,
the justices who who wrote the dissenting

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:30.280
opinion say when people say the types of things
you're saying, you're fanning political flames

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.739
in a way that is not good.

00:03:31.739 --> 00:03:34.390
You're making people think you're talking
about fraudulent results.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:37.650
When all they said was some of these weren't
accessible to people with disabilities, that's

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:38.879
all they said.

00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:42.849
Speaker 3: So what what they said, if you
read the final decision.

00:03:42.849 --> 00:03:43.849
Yes.

00:03:43.849 --> 00:03:47.400
That the method of delivery is to observe.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.400
Right.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.420
So there's two methods of delivery in the
statute.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:52.420
Yes.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:58.270
Wisconsin Election Commission introduced a
third without going through this, the legislature.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:02.069
So that to me is an extremely scary position
to be in.

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:08.180
The Supreme Court said that those those boxes
that were used were not under the method of

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:11.430
which deliberation was described in the Constitution.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:12.760
So you are correct.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:16.570
I'm not trying to say that the Supreme Court
said the elections of ballot, none of that

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:17.570
good.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:20.950
Speaker 1: They just said they should have
all been accessible to individuals with disabilities.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:21.950
Yep.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:22.950
So.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:23.950
Okay.

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:28.830
Speaker 3: The next thing that I'd like to
point out is we had Justice Gable on 139 page

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:29.830
report.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:33.160
And I would really like for that report to
be put before a judge.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:37.990
And I would like the judicial system to actually
make a ruling on the findings of that report.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:38.990
Okay.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:44.060
So the Wisconsin Election Commission decided
to remove special voting deputies, right?

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:47.350
They said they were not necessary in 2020.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:52.130
And I believe from my bottom of my heart,
my great grandmother died at 102 at our house

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:54.140
that they were.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:55.699
That they were removed.

00:04:55.699 --> 00:04:59.410
And we had people that were executing the
elections and collecting ballots that were

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:00.720
not under the constitution.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:01.720
They were not followed.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:02.720
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.919
So you've you've raised a couple of concerns
that that's fair.

00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:11.650
And but you're also conceding that on that
first one, it's really about disability rights.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:13.199
Let me ask you this.

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:16.180
What is a forensic election audit?

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:20.980
Because and the reason I ask is everybody
I ask has a different explanation.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:25.650
And when I point to audits that have that
have been done, folks will invariably say

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.630
that's not really the type of audit I'm talking
about.

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:29.900
Speaker 3: Hmm.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:30.900
Got it.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.889
That's a very fair question, because you're
exactly right, David.

00:05:33.889 --> 00:05:35.229
Let's go across the country.

00:05:35.229 --> 00:05:36.229
Right.

00:05:36.229 --> 00:05:37.229
You look at Arizona.

00:05:37.229 --> 00:05:38.229
Yeah.

00:05:38.229 --> 00:05:39.509
And look at Georgia.

00:05:39.509 --> 00:05:40.540
Even here in Wisconsin.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:45.110
The the audit that that we'd like to find
out is what happens to your ballot when it

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:46.259
goes through the machine.

00:05:46.259 --> 00:05:47.919
I'm not telling you the machines are fraudulent.

00:05:47.919 --> 00:05:49.190
I can't tell you that, David.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:50.190
You can't tell me that.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:51.190
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:54.050
I certainly can't tell you the machines are
fraudulent because I have no reason to believe

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:55.050
that.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:56.050
Yeah.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:57.050
Speaker 3: And.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:58.050
And I can't either.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:05:59.050
Right.

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:00.050
So good.

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:01.979
Can I. Can I maybe reframe this discussion
a little bit so.

00:06:01.979 --> 00:06:03.479
Speaker 1: You can reframe it?

00:06:03.479 --> 00:06:06.340
But I would love an answer to what you mean
by a forensic election.

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:07.340
All right.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:08.340
Speaker 3: I apologize.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:13.180
So a forensic audit is when when your ballot
is cast, right?

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:14.180
Yes.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:16.410
The process by which your ballot is cast.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:21.430
Once that ballot goes into whether it's scanned
or a machine goes into a box in a county,

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:23.710
courthouse gets counted.

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:24.710
Yes.

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:25.710
And the total results are finished.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:29.330
I believe that process should be clear to
the public.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.919
So a forensic audit to me is understanding
every step of that process without equivocation.

00:06:33.919 --> 00:06:34.919
Okay.

00:06:34.919 --> 00:06:36.770
Speaker 1: Now, let me see if I understand
it.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:38.760
And you tell me the elements that are missing.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:39.760
Right.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:43.530
Because I've voted with these types of machines
before.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:45.610
I have a ballot.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:46.789
I have a ballot.

00:06:46.789 --> 00:06:52.810
I take my felt sharpie and I sort of like
connect to little black things and make it

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:54.530
a fixed line.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:57.849
I feed it in through a machine.

00:06:57.849 --> 00:07:04.260
Presumably the machine identifies which line
I drew and credits that candidate with a vote.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:10.970
And then my ballot goes into a pile, and then
later you could go back in and count up the

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:16.070
pieces of paper with the lines and compare
it to the results from the machine.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.050
Is that a fair process?

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:20.710
Speaker 3: That that process.

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:21.710
Yeah.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:23.160
Is that part of the audit?

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:24.160
Okay.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:25.160
Exactly.

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:26.160
Right.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.160
Yeah.

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:28.160
Okay.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.160
Speaker 1: And what else is there?

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:35.030
Speaker 3: Well, for me in Wisconsin, we have
a process called early absentee voting, and

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:42.320
that's 47 days that starts September 22nd
for the general election, in my opinion.

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:47.090
Based on the I'd say the evidence, if you
will, that has never been presented before

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:51.919
a court that we have men and women here in
Wisconsin.

00:07:51.919 --> 00:07:55.860
There's a there was a case yesterday that
they heard the DOJ brought against a man by

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:57.830
the name of Harry Wade.

00:07:57.830 --> 00:08:03.949
Harry Waite requested a ballot, an absentee
ballot for five people with their birth date

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:05.300
in their first and last name.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:08.880
That's the only thing you need to request
their ballot if they voted absentee in the

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:09.880
past.

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:11.069
There's no I.D.

00:08:11.069 --> 00:08:12.110
required.

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:15.639
Because they've already submitted a request
for an absentee ballot.

00:08:15.639 --> 00:08:20.220
The problem that I have is, let's say that
what you and I just described as perfectly

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:21.220
flawless.

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:22.220
Right.

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:23.220
I'll concede that.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:25.259
Let's say it's perfectly flawless.

00:08:25.259 --> 00:08:29.889
If you and I would like to request ballots
from an individual who has voted one out of

00:08:29.889 --> 00:08:37.159
the last 15 election cycles, and we submit
it by an absentee ballot in a mailbox or drop.

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:42.740
There is no way for you and I. Based on the
process you just defined.

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:46.510
To confirm or deny whether or not that ballot
was cast by that individual.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:50.029
Speaker 1: So a few things on that because
I've heard and read about this many, many

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:51.029
times before.

00:08:51.029 --> 00:08:52.440
There's a few different things.

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:57.990
Number one is, even if what you're saying
is true in theory, someone could do that.

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:01.350
I could find someone who hasn't voted in a
really long time.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:02.350
I'm admitting you're right.

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:07.010
I could I could try to figure out who hasn't
voted in a really long time, request the ballot

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:09.770
in their name, and then fill it out and send
it in.

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:10.770
Fine.

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:14.320
In order for us to be able to say it's worth.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:16.690
X, Y or Z.

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:18.470
Audit, investigation, whatever.

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:23.130
I would want to, number one, have evidence
that that happened in numbers that could have

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.260
swayed the results.

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:30.070
And in order to believe that, I would have
to have reason to think that one side rather

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:33.940
than the other was disproportionately doing
it.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:40.040
Because even if I grant that what you're saying
could be done and was done, if it was done

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:44.790
equally by Republicans and Democrats, it doesn't
actually change the outcome, does it?

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:46.670
Does my math check out at least Adam.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:51.560
In other words, if 10,000 Republicans do that
and 10,000 Democrats do that with these people

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:55.060
who haven't voted in a while, Biden still
wins by 20,000 votes.

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.060
Right?

00:09:56.060 --> 00:10:00.240
The math checks out at least.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.149
Oh.

00:10:04.149 --> 00:10:06.029
And is he was that question too tough?

00:10:06.029 --> 00:10:08.430
I can't believe that was why he left.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:10.990
Adam, where are you?

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:14.950
Oh, my.

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:21.630
I think he bailed because of that question.

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:24.420
What on earth?

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:26.790
Adam.

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:28.829
Don't tell me that question was too difficult.

00:10:28.829 --> 00:10:30.300
Adam, you're back.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.079
Speaker 3: We were on hold.

00:10:32.079 --> 00:10:33.079
Speaker 1: Yeah, you put.

00:10:33.079 --> 00:10:35.670
I thought you bailed because that you didn't
like the question.

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:36.860
Speaker 3: No, sir.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:37.860
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:40.110
So my question was, I'm glad you're back.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:45.589
My question was, if 10,000 Democrats and 10,000
Republicans do the thing you described, it

00:10:45.589 --> 00:10:47.670
doesn't change the margin of victory.

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:54.170
So the question I have is, what evidence do
we have that what you just described as possible

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:59.180
was not only done, but was done by so many
more Democrats than Republicans that it would

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:00.959
change the outcome in Wisconsin.

00:11:00.959 --> 00:11:05.970
Speaker 3: I would like to contend that your
premise of your question is wrong.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:06.970
Okay.

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:07.970
Well, I agree with your question.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:09.100
Speaker 1: Tell me how it's wrong.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:13.380
Speaker 3: Well, so my grandfather was in
the 82nd Airborne Division.

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:17.089
We have men and women who have died for this
country so that we can have the right to vote

00:11:17.089 --> 00:11:18.160
yes.

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:23.760
I personally believe that if you use the line
of questioning that you've so chosen that

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:25.200
if both sides do it, it's okay.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.380
Speaker 1: That's not the that's not what
I said, though.

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:28.380
Speaker 3: Okay.

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:30.490
Speaker 1: So it's a two part question.

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:31.600
First, go ahead.

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:35.750
What evidence do we have that this was done
in large numbers at all?

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:38.019
Because that's been investigated in Wisconsin.

00:11:38.019 --> 00:11:43.670
And part two is, if it was done in large numbers,
was it done disproportionately by one side?

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:49.280
Speaker 3: So I would love absolutely love
to hear, first of all, the premise of the

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:54.070
investigation that happened, because this
is the perfect crime, David, for you to say

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:59.600
that someone voted absentee and their ballot
was determined that they actually did vote

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:05.360
absentee and it was found that they really
didn't vote because that would take a canvass.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:09.850
When I say a full forensic audit that would
take a canvass of the people that actually

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:11.230
were elected or voted in this election.

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:15.160
Speaker 1: So you're saying go door knocking
all the people who supposedly voted and say,

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:16.160
did you really vote?

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:17.230
Speaker 3: Well, no, no, no.

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:18.980
I'm asking your question.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:21.779
You said that that was investigative police.

00:12:21.779 --> 00:12:24.350
Tell me, how was that investigated?

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:27.980
Because that's the only method I know to verify
what you just said.

